@page "/components/codes"
@using Stil.Docs.Examples.Codes

<DocsHeader Title="QR codes and barcodes" Eyebrow="Capture">
    <Lead>Codes for badges, parcels, tickets and products, drawn as SVG in C#.</Lead>
</DocsHeader>

<p>
    <code>StilQrCode</code> and <code>StilBarcode</code> draw their code on the server, with no JavaScript and no
    third-party code, so they show in static SSR, scale without blur and print sharply. A code is dark on light in both
    color modes, because scanners expect that; set <code>--stil-barcode-color</code> and <code>--stil-barcode-bg</code>
    to change it, but keep the contrast high. Each code is an image named by its value, or by <code>Label</code>. A
    value the format cannot hold shows a short message instead.
</p>

<h2 id="qr">QR codes</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(QrCodes)">
    <p>
        <code>Value</code> is any text: a link, an ID or a vCard. Digits and upper-case text make the smallest codes.
        <code>ErrorCorrection</code> (Low, Medium, Quartile, High) sets how much of the code can be damaged and still
        read; more makes a denser code. <code>Size</code> sets the width and height; the default is 10rem.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="barcodes">Barcodes</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Barcodes)">
    <p>
        <code>Format</code> is <code>Code128</code> (any printable ASCII, the default), <code>Ean13</code> or
        <code>Ean8</code>. EAN takes the digits with or without the check digit; without it, the check digit is added,
        and a wrong one is refused. <code>ShowText</code> prints the value under the bars, and <code>Width</code> sets
        the width; the height follows.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="without-a-component">Without a component</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(CodesFromCode)">
    <p>
        <code>StilCodes</code> has the encoders: <code>QrSvg</code> and <code>BarcodeSvg</code> return an SVG document
        (black on white unless you pass colors), and <code>QrModules</code> and <code>BarcodeModules</code> return the
        modules, for a PDF library or a label printer. <code>EanCheckDigit</code> computes an EAN check digit.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="printing">Printing and scanning</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Keep the light margin around a code: it is part of the code (four modules for QR, ten for barcodes).</li>
    <li>Print at least 2 cm wide for a QR code and 3.5 cm for a barcode, and test with the scanners you use.</li>
    <li>For codes that get dirty or worn, use <code>ErrorCorrection="StilQrErrorCorrection.High"</code>.</li>
</ul>

